<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Salem</h1><p class="st-meta">1354 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Salem</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A wealthy Cistercian abbey near Lake Constance whose abbots ruled one of the largest monastic territories in Swabia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-baden-wurttemberg">Salem</a> was founded in the 1130s by the knight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a> of Adelsreute as a Cistercian house settled by monks from Lucelle. It grew rapidly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> protection, and in 1354 Charles IV confirmed its direct dependence on the empire. Its status was contested for centuries by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and others, but its abbots sat among the prelates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbots ruled a compact territory of villages north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> through their own courts and officials, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-baden-wurttemberg">Salem</a> one of the richest imperial abbeys in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Swabia</a>, with vineyards, granges and town houses in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-constance">Konstanz</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-uberlingen">Überlingen</a>. Its great Gothic minster dates from around 1300, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> abbey buildings were rebuilt after a fire in 1697.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 and 1803 the abbey was secularised and granted to the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, who turned it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-baden-wurttemberg">Salem</a> Castle, still owned by the family. In 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-hahn">Kurt Hahn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Max of Baden</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schule-schloss-salem">Schule Schloss Salem</a> boarding school there.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-salem.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Salem" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salem_Front_NO.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1354 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salem</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Salem Abbey, Salmansweiler</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-salem" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Salem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4897 504 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Salem lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5217 760L5229 694M5217 760L5194 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5194 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5217 760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5217 760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Salem. <a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-salem">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
